    <h2 id="where-meta-asks">Where Meta Asks for a Privacy Policy URL</h2>

    <p>Meta checks for a valid privacy policy URL at multiple points in the app lifecycle. Missing it at any stage blocks your progress.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>App Dashboard &gt; Settings &gt; Basic.</strong> The <code>Privacy Policy URL</code> field appears directly below the <code>App Domains</code> field on the Basic Settings page. This is the primary location where Meta reads your policy URL. The field accepts any publicly accessible HTTPS URL -- it cannot be blank, cannot point to <code>localhost</code>, and cannot sit behind authentication. Meta's crawler must be able to reach it and receive an HTTP 200 response. Use the <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/" rel="nofollow">Sharing Debugger</a> to confirm the URL resolves correctly before saving.</li>

      <li><strong>App Review submission.</strong> When you submit permissions or features for App Review at <code>App Dashboard &gt; App Review &gt; Permissions and Features</code>, Meta's review team visits your privacy policy URL as part of their evaluation. The review checks that the policy matches the data your app actually accesses through the permissions you requested. A policy that does not mention the specific permissions under review will be rejected.</li>

      <li><strong>Business Verification.</strong> Apps requesting advanced access to certain permissions must complete Business Verification through the <code>App Dashboard &gt; Settings &gt; Advanced &gt; Business Verification</code> section. The verification process requires a legal entity with a privacy policy URL that matches the business identity. The policy must be hosted on a domain the business controls or on a verified website.</li>

      <li><strong>Data Use Checkup.</strong> Meta periodically requires apps to complete a Data Use Checkup through a banner notification in the App Dashboard. The checkup asks developers to re-confirm how they use each approved permission and verifies that the privacy policy URL is still active. Apps that fail the checkup or let it expire have their permissions revoked.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="platform-terms">What Meta's Platform Terms Require the Policy to Disclose</h2>

    <p>Section 4 of Meta's <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/terms/" rel="nofollow">Platform Terms</a> sets out the privacy policy requirements. The policy must be available at a publicly accessible URL, cannot be geo-blocked, and must be clearly labeled as a privacy policy. It must be your own policy, not another company's boilerplate. Meta summarizes the requirements in their <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/development/terms-and-policies/privacy-policy/" rel="nofollow">Privacy Policy Expectations</a> documentation.</p>

    <p>The policy must disclose:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>What data is collected.</strong> Every category of information your app collects from or about users, whether obtained directly through Facebook's APIs, through third-party SDKs, or gathered independently (browser information, IP addresses, device identifiers, server logs). If your app requests the <code>email</code> permission, your policy must state that you collect email addresses from Facebook. If you request <code>user_birthday</code>, you must disclose that you collect date of birth.</li>

      <li><strong>How the data is used.</strong> Each specific purpose for processing the data. Meta expects concrete descriptions -- "to personalize content recommendations," "to send weekly summary emails," "to match users with relevant groups" -- not vague catch-alls like "to improve our services." The purpose must match what you described in your App Review submission.</li>

      <li><strong>How users can request deletion.</strong> A working method for users to request that their data be deleted. This can be an email address, a contact form, or, for apps that support it, Meta's <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/development/create-an-app/app-dashboard/data-deletion-callback" rel="nofollow">Data Deletion Request Callback</a>. The URL or contact method you provide must be active and monitored -- Meta's review team tests it. If your app implements the Data Deletion Callback, configure it at <code>App Dashboard &gt; Settings &gt; Basic &gt; Data Deletion</code> with either a callback URL or a data deletion instructions URL.</li>

      <li><strong>Third-party sharing.</strong> Whether data obtained through Facebook's APIs is shared with any third party, and if so, which parties and for what purposes. Meta's Platform Terms prohibit transferring data received through their APIs to any data broker, ad network, or data reseller, even if your policy discloses it.</li>

      <li><strong>Data retention.</strong> How long you keep data obtained through the APIs. When a user removes your app or when Meta sends an <code>app_deauthorize</code> callback, you must delete all data received through their APIs within a reasonable period unless the data has been aggregated and anonymized.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A privacy policy that fails to cover these points is treated as incomplete during App Review. <a href="/resources/how-to-write-privacy-policy-for-app/">Writing a complete privacy policy for any app</a> starts with auditing which data your app actually touches, not guessing from the code.</p>

    <h2 id="permissions-data">Facebook Login and Graph API Permissions: Data Each Returns</h2>

    <p>Every permission your app requests through Facebook Login maps directly to a category of user data your policy must disclose. The table below covers the most common permissions. Review your app's permission list at <code>App Dashboard &gt; App Review &gt; Permissions and Features</code> and cross-reference it against your policy.</p>

