        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>read_insights</code></td>
          <td>Analytics and insights data for Pages, apps, and web domains the user owns</td>
          <td>Insights and analytics data</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>If your app requests a permission, your privacy policy must name the data that permission grants access to. Apps that request permissions they do not use will be rejected during App Review -- Meta expects a one-to-one match between requested permissions and stated functionality. Generate the data-collection disclosures for any third-party SDKs your app bundles with the <a href="/tools/sdk-disclosure-generator/">SDK disclosure generator</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="pages-lead-ads">Facebook Pages and Lead Ads Privacy Requirements</h2>

    <p>Facebook Pages and Lead Ads have their own privacy policy requirements that exist independently of the App Review process.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Facebook Pages.</strong> Any Page that collects information from visitors -- through messaging, Lead Ads, or Shop features -- must link to a privacy policy. For Pages managed through a third-party app, the app's privacy policy must also disclose that it accesses Page data. The <code>pages_read_engagement</code> scope gives access to follower PSIDs (Page-Scoped User IDs) and profile pictures, which are personal data under GDPR even though they differ from the user's global Facebook ID.</li>

      <li><strong>Lead Ads.</strong> When you create a Lead Ad form in Ads Manager, the <code>Privacy Policy</code> field is mandatory. The form cannot be published without a valid URL. The URL appears as a clickable link at the bottom of the lead form, directly above the submit button. Users must be able to read the policy before submitting their information. Lead Ad forms collect whatever custom fields you define -- name, email, phone number, job title, company -- and your policy must disclose each one. If you use the Conversions API (<code>server_events</code>) or the Meta Pixel to send event data back to Meta, those data flows must also be disclosed.</li>

      <li><strong>Custom Audiences.</strong> If your app uploads customer lists to create Custom Audiences (via <code>ads_management</code> or the Marketing API), your privacy policy must disclose that you share user data with Meta for advertising purposes. This includes hashed email addresses, phone numbers, and any other identifiers used for matching.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="rejection-reasons">Why App Review Rejects a Privacy Policy URL</h2>

    <p>Meta's App Review team rejects privacy policy submissions for specific, fixable reasons. These are the most common rejection patterns based on publicly documented review feedback and developer community reports:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>"The privacy policy URL provided is not accessible."</strong> The URL returns a 404, 403, 500, or times out. Meta's crawler cannot reach pages behind authentication, VPNs, or geo-restrictions. Before submitting, test the URL in the <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/" rel="nofollow">Sharing Debugger</a> -- if the debugger cannot scrape the page, the review team cannot read it either. The URL must load over HTTPS without redirecting through a login page.</li>

      <li><strong>"Your app's privacy policy must clearly explain the data you are collecting and how you are using it."</strong> The policy exists but does not mention Facebook-specific data types. A generic template that says "we collect personal information" without naming <code>public_profile</code> fields, email addresses obtained through Facebook Login, or Graph API data will be flagged. The fix is to list each requested permission and the specific data it accesses, as shown in the permissions table above.</li>

      <li><strong>"The privacy policy does not explain how users can request deletion of their data."</strong> The policy lacks a deletion mechanism or the contact information is missing. Provide a working email address, a link to a contact form, or configure the Data Deletion Request Callback at <code>App Dashboard &gt; Settings &gt; Basic &gt; Data Deletion</code>.</li>

      <li><strong>"The privacy policy URL does not load a page that is clearly a privacy policy."</strong> The URL points to a homepage, an "About" page, or a terms-of-service page that buries privacy information in a larger document. The page must be clearly titled "Privacy Policy" and dedicated entirely to privacy disclosures.</li>

      <li><strong>"We were unable to test your app."</strong> This is not strictly a privacy policy rejection, but it blocks the entire submission. If the review team cannot log in, navigate, or exercise the features that use the requested permissions, the full submission fails. Provide test credentials and step-by-step instructions in the <code>App Review &gt; Submissions</code> notes field.</li>

      <li><strong>Policy hosted on a non-HTTPS URL.</strong> Meta requires the privacy policy URL to load over HTTPS. HTTP-only URLs are rejected outright. Free hosting services that do not provide TLS certificates will not work.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Each of these is documented in the rejection feedback email Meta sends after a failed review. Fix the specific issue cited, resubmit, and the turnaround for re-review is typically 1 to 5 business days.</p>

    <h2 id="disclosure-template">Copy-Ready Facebook App Disclosure Block</h2>

    <p>Add this block to your existing privacy policy to cover your app's Facebook integration. Replace the bracketed fields with your actual data practices and remove any sections that do not apply.</p>

    <div class="copy-block">
      <div class="copy-block-header">
        <span class="copy-block-label">Facebook App Privacy Disclosure &mdash; ready to copy</span>
      </div>
      <div class="copy-block-body" id="disclosure-text">
<p><strong>Facebook Integration</strong></p>

<p>Our application integrates with Facebook's platform through the Graph API and Facebook Login. This section describes the data we receive through these integrations.</p>

<p><strong>Data We Collect Through Facebook Login</strong></p>
<p>When you log in with Facebook, we receive and store the following information from your Facebook account, based on the permissions you grant:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Basic profile information</em> (granted automatically) &mdash; your user ID, first name, last name, and profile picture URL.</li>
<li><em>Email address</em> [if email permission is requested] &mdash; the primary email address associated with your Facebook account.</li>
<li>[Add additional permissions as applicable, e.g.: <em>Date of birth</em> (user_birthday permission), <em>Friends list</em> (user_friends permission &mdash; limited to friends who also use this app).]</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>How We Use Facebook Data</strong></p>
<p>We use the data received through Facebook's APIs to: [describe each specific purpose, e.g., authenticate your identity and create your account / personalize your experience / display your profile information within the app]. We do not use Facebook data for advertising, sell it to third parties, or share it with data brokers.</p>

<p><strong>Data Deletion</strong></p>
<p>You may request deletion of all data we received through Facebook by [contacting us at your-email@example.com / using the deletion form at (URL) / revoking the app's access in your Facebook Settings &gt; Apps and Websites, which triggers our automated deletion process via Meta's Data Deletion Request Callback]. We process deletion requests within [number] days.</p>

<p><strong>Third-Party Services Receiving Facebook Data</strong></p>
<p>[Name each service and what it receives, e.g.: <strong>AWS</strong> hosts our servers and stores your Facebook profile data. <strong>Sentry</strong> receives error logs that may include your user ID. No other third party receives data obtained through Facebook's APIs.]</p>

<p><strong>Data Retention</strong></p>
<p>Data received through Facebook's APIs is retained for [number] days while your account is active. When you delete your account or revoke our app's access through Facebook, we delete all Facebook-obtained data within [number] days.</p>
      </div>
      <button class="copy-btn" id="copy-disclosure-btn" type="button">Copy to clipboard</button>
    </div>

    <p>Need a complete privacy policy rather than just the Facebook section? <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">Use the free privacy policy generator</a> to build a full policy that covers GDPR, CCPA, and app store requirements, then host it at a permanent URL you can paste into your App Dashboard.</p>

